<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Purcell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1659–<a href="/years/1695">1695</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Purcell was an English composer and organist of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> era whose music, English in style, incorporated Italian and French elements. A former chorister of the Chapel Royal, he became organist of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> in 1679, when John Blow resigned the office in his favour, and organist of the Chapel Royal in 1682. His works include more than 100 songs, the opera <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a>, the semi-operas Dioclesian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-arthur">King Arthur</a> and The Fairy-Queen, odes such as Come Ye Sons of Art, and music for the funeral of Queen Mary in 1695. He died at his home in Marsham Street in November 1695, at the height of his career, and is buried beside the organ in Westminster Abbey.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-purcell.jpg" alt="Henry Purcell" width="330" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Closterman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Henry_Purcell_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry Purcell was born in Westminster in 1659, in St Ann's Lane, and the family lived a few hundred yards from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>. His father, also named Henry, died in 1664, and the child was placed under the guardianship of his uncle Thomas, a gentleman of the Chapel Royal. Thomas arranged for him to become a chorister, and he studied under Captain Henry Cooke and then Pelham Humfrey, a pupil of Lully. The composer Matthew Locke was a family friend.</p><p>When his voice broke in 1673 he left the Chapel Royal and became assistant to the organ-builder John Hingston. The earliest work identified as his with certainty is an ode for the King's birthday written in 1670, when he was eleven. After Humfrey's death he studied under John Blow, was a pupil at Westminster School, and in 1676 was appointed copyist at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1679 Blow resigned the post of organist of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> in Purcell's favour, and he devoted himself almost entirely to sacred music for six years. Soon after his marriage in 1682 he became organist of the Chapel Royal as well. In 1685 he wrote the anthems I was glad and My heart is inditing for the coronation of King James II, and in 1694 he composed Come Ye Sons of Art, one of his most elaborate works, to a birthday ode by Nahum Tate for Queen Mary.</p><p>He returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in 1687 with music for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-dryden">John Dryden</a>'s Tyrannick Love. His later stage works included Dioclesian in 1690, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-arthur">King Arthur</a> in 1691 and The Fairy-Queen in 1692, an adaptation of Shakespeare's A Midsummer Night's Dream whose score was rediscovered in 1901. His chamber opera <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a>, to a libretto by Nahum Tate, was written for private performance. In his last six years he wrote music for forty-two plays. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/te-deum">Te Deum</a> and Jubilate Deo of 1694 was the first English Te Deum with orchestral accompaniment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Purcell died on 21 November 1695 at his home in Marsham Street, at the height of his career, aged about 35 or 36. The cause is unclear: one theory is that he caught a chill, another that he died of tuberculosis. He was buried in the north aisle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> beside the organ, and the music he had written for the funeral of Queen Mary was performed at his own. His widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">Frances</a> published many of his works, including the collection Orpheus Britannicus.</p><p>John Blow wrote an ode on his death, and later composers admired him. His setting of Thou knowest Lord has been sung at every British state funeral since William Croft preserved it in 1724. Benjamin Britten arranged many of his vocal works. A Purcell Society, founded in 1876, published new editions of his music.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry Purcell” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It has been retold on the stage and screen: <b>Henry Purcell</b>'s opera Dido and Aeneas of 1688 with its lament When I am laid in earth, Hector Berlioz's Les Troyens, Italian film and television versions, and Brian Friel's play Translations, which ends with a passage from it.</blockquote><a href="/work/aeneid">Aeneid · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The music writer Percy Scholes found likenesses to an early plainsong melody, to galliard dance tunes, to a keyboard piece by John Bull (1619) and to pieces by <b>Henry Purcell</b>, and recommended crediting the tune as &quot;traditional&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a></li><li><blockquote>The melody of &quot;Lillibullero&quot; is attributed to <b>Henry Purcell</b>, though it is unclear whether he composed it or arranged it; it was first published in The Delightful Companion, John Playford's method book for the recorder, in 1686, and no original manuscript survives.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lillibullero">Lillibullero</a></li><li><blockquote>A Voluntary on the Old Hundredth for organ is ascribed to John Blow or to his pupil <b>Henry Purcell</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/old-100th">Old 100th</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Westminster, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Westminster, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.5,-0.13&amp;year=1682" aria-label="Westminster on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry Purcell"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.5,-0.13&amp;year=1682">What was here in 1682? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
